    <Label Name="Title" Style="Heading" Text="The Haberdasher's Puzzle" Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="16" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
    <Label Name="Description" Style="Explanation" Text="Cut an equilateral triangle into four pieces that fit together into a square. Drag the slider: the pieces swing on three hinges, like links of a bracelet, and the triangle becomes the square. Swing them back and it is a triangle again.\n\nHenry Dudeney, an English puzzle maker, set this problem in 1902 and asked for the fewest pieces. His own answer took five (see Triangle to Square in Five Pieces). A reader, C. W. McElroy, sent in this one with four, and Dudeney liked it so much that he had a model made of mahogany with brass hinges. For a hundred years nobody knew whether three pieces could do it. In 2024 it was proved that they can't.\n\nThe cuts hide a surprise. In the square, the three corners of the triangle meet at one point on a cut, and it looks like the middle of that cut. It isn't quite: the two parts are 51 and 49 hundredths of it. A model made by guessing the middle never closes." Pin="TopRight" OffsetX="16" OffsetY="64" WrapWidth="400" Backdrop="true" />
